physics.pdf]]"],[17122,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[17230,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[17236,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[17239,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[17242,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[17680,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[18547,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[18577,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[18724,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[18751,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[19519,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[19705,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[19732,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[19834,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[19858,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[20302,"[[foundationsofphysics.pdf]]"],[21262,"[[Fractions and Decimal Forms]] extends division beyond the natural numbers. A fraction records a numerator divided by a nonzero denominator. Different numerator-denominator pairs can represent the same point on the number line, so the cancellation law explains equivalent fractions and reduction to lowest terms. Addition and subtraction require a common denominator, while multiplication acts directly on numerators and denominators. Division becomes multiplication by the reciprocal.\n\nDecimal form connects fractions to positional notation. A rational number has a decimal expansion that terminates or repeats, and a repeating decimal can be converted back to a fraction by aligning and subtracting repeated tails. An irrational number instead has an infinite nonrepeating expansion. These representations show why exact equality must be distinguished from approximation: a short decimal can approximate an irrational quantity without becoming equal to it."],[438,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[453,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[459,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[789,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[1248,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[1896,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[1905,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[1908,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[2478,"[[frontiersofdatascience.pdf]]"],[3824,"[[frontiersofdatascience.